==============================================================================================
A  DRIFT vs NOISE -- what does a REAL trend score, at each horizon?
==============================================================================================
   A market that moves X% over one 84-bar session, with 5-minute sigma of 0.05%,
   has per-bar drift mu = X/84.  Simulated, 20,000 blocks a cell.

   mu/sigma  daily move    H=8      H=20     H=42     H=83    <- mean z (0.5 = no signal)
      0.000      0.0%    0.5010   0.4976   0.4992   0.4965
      0.119      0.5%    0.5211   0.5420   0.5865   0.6564
      0.238      1.0%    0.5651   0.6448   0.7575   0.8827
      0.476      2.0%    0.7075   0.8666   0.9676   0.9973
      0.952      4.0%    0.9213   0.9952   1.0000   1.0000

   READ THE TOP-LEFT AGAINST THE BOTTOM-RIGHT. That is the defect: a genuine trend is
   INVISIBLE at the horizons Stage 0 swept, and obvious only at the session scale.

==============================================================================================
B  THE RETRACEMENT PROBLEM -- eff demands monotonicity; real moves retrace ~50% (D471)
==============================================================================================
   path                              net   pathlen    eff      z      verdict
   straight line, 8 up                 +8         8   1.000  0.995   TRENDING
   trend, 25% retrace (+3,-1)x4        +8        16   0.500  0.793   ordinary
   trend, 50% retrace (+2,-1)x4        +4        12   0.333  0.607   ordinary
   trend, one big leg then chop        +7        13   0.538  0.636   ordinary
   pure chop (+1,-1)x4                 +0         8   0.000  0.135   REVERTING

   D471 measured adverse/|move| = 0.45-0.50 in EVERY cell: a winning move goes about half
   its size against you first. THAT path is the '50% retrace' row -- and eff calls it
   ordinary, because the sign shuffle can rearrange the same magnitudes into a straight line.

